    web_documents {
      url: "https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHAq45k37fDqFm_IOYOB5NxB8bAp3v8jDphUyglUCRdRHcJodpu0bSrn3UlcN9VOL8vYrbMrreW-HkCx_x5FeKNfjFZ8NTRxAKBEBLcEEVuPXzr8hc2JrSBBghsvuSsTxuelX299w=="
      title: "youtube.com"
    }
    summary: "The search query `site:unsplash.com/photos baker OR baking OR \"kneading dough\"` was executed to find relevant images on Unsplash. While the search results provided general information about baking and kneading dough, they did not directly provide image results from Unsplash.com.\n\nThe search results discuss various aspects of baking, including the role of a baker, the process and importance of kneading dough, and tips for successful baking. A baker is a tradesperson who bakes and sometimes sells flour-based products, working in a place called a bakery[1]. Their duties typically include checking ingredient quality, preparing equipment, measuring ingredients, combining them, and then kneading, rolling, cutting, and shaping dough[1].\n\nKneading is a crucial step in yeast bread baking, where ingredients are worked together to form dough, evenly mix ingredients, and develop the interior structure (crumb)[2][3]. During kneading, two proteins in flour, gliadin and glutenin, combine to form gluten strands. Kneading warms these strands, allowing them to expand during fermentation and bond, resulting in a more elastic dough with better structure[3]. Kneading also distributes yeast and carbon dioxide bubbles, creating a consistent crumb and uniform proofing and baking[3].\n\nTips for kneading dough by hand include lightly flouring hands and the work surface, pushing the dough away with the heels of the hands in a rocking motion, and using just enough flour to prevent excessive sticking[2]. The dough should still feel slightly tacky[2]. The texture of the dough will change as you knead, becoming smoother, softer, and more elastic[2]. The \"windowpane test\" can determine if the dough is sufficiently kneaded: a small piece of dough should stretch thin enough to see light through without tearing[4]. Over-kneading can lead to a tough, stiff dough[3]. Some doughs, especially high-hydration ones, may require a \"stretch and fold\" method rather than traditional kneading[5].\n\nSources:\n[1] [wikipedia.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGMn-1LX8IpXfgMhcKw9O7lFreUrgxSyPApnWA2D12qoCfP9FuH2_noEo8UaL3klCj7GiLKMpkx75vzGyTtHywGQnXNqdPz69vuOa6a6NL6zE-rRGqT23ga79EG3-k=)\n[2] [sallysbakingaddiction.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEJt-TgBBAKNkHyIvRnbiQXxoSNb7JyXvv6BU1Ko9l2T5HltoZ6oDgXyGWeACxDQQIURGS4fsNMpMpNtXNXPpnIhtZAFIyyN7T7fWIdxP-F7Mz45Sgg6renF0Jvcu6iFFxiIatxa8jun6-zvfmyGIk=)\n[3] [masterclass.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEpXgVkW9xqfgd7gPCbS8BE2qYpHwRfAVQswUA13G2E4Sab6YZrcGxJg5wuR90GffGxlA70_aRo_AvHat7p5CkQMPncL24gVJ0IspVIBXQjqUv1xjcvzmUvvJQwhBx4dycKBNux-_F1UOzSSMscxxs6HlRJOKBIuQ==)\n[4] [amybakesbread.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEviNaQcZ7gUyfTUXx8YgGSgXcE0l_ossTzedKgc96xHSl7dfo4RU9lgbfb2pA4lXgOGYRFxzVchdCVlDnBaLL_AcQdXklzS_VhwVIl6Kp9IwycKVlznzx0GlQcil_MIfYpmJkghtlmFrzAXWkO23aikxiS)\n[5] [youtube.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHAq45k37fDqFm_IOYOB5NxB8bAp3v8jDphUyglUCRdRHcJodpu0bSrn3UlcN9VOL8vYrbMrreW-HkCx_x5FeKNfjFZ8NTRxAKBEBLcEEVuPXzr8hc2JrSBBghsvuSsTxuelX299w==)"
