    summary: "Unsplash offers a wide variety of free, high-resolution \"brownie\" photos that are available for commercial use without attribution[1][2][3][4][5]. You can find over 100+ \"brownie\" images and over 100+ \"chocolate brownies\" images on the platform[1][2][3][4][5].\n\nWhen it comes to photographing brownies and other brown foods for platforms like Unsplash, there are several tips to consider for creating appealing \"photo-\" quality images:\n*   **Cool and Chill Brownies** Brownies photograph better when cold as they hold their shape, preventing a messy look. Chilling them overnight in the fridge or 20 minutes in the freezer is recommended[6].\n*   **Use a Hot Knife** To achieve sharp, even edges when cutting brownies, use a hot knife[6].\n*   **Consider Color Palette** Brownies are brown, so using a contrasting backdrop, like white, and simple props can make them stand out. Adding lighter elements, such as a caramel center, can also add interest[6].\n*   **Utilize Dutch-Processed Cocoa Powder** Dutch-processed cocoa powder can give brownies a true brown color rather than a red tinge, which photographs well[6].\n*   **Stack Them** Stacking brownies adds height and interest, showcasing their rich, gooey profile, especially when photographed from an angle that highlights their layers. Topping them with ice cream or chocolate can also enhance their appeal[6].\n*   **Capture Texture** Emphasizing texture is crucial for brown foods. Using clever lighting, such as positioning the light to pull out texture, can make the food look crunchy or crumbly[7]. Macro lenses can also help in getting close-up shots to highlight texture[8].\n*   **Change Perspective** Experiment with different camera angles (from above, below, or even cutting the brownie open to reveal the inside) to find the most interesting shot[7].\n*   **Use Specular Highlights** To add life and detail to reflective subjects like chocolate, create specular highlights (direct reflections of your light source)[7].\n*   **Hard Light for Shape** Hard light can create defined shadows, adding dimension and shaping the texture of the food, which is particularly useful for subjects that aren\'t inherently \"pretty\"[8].\n*   **Minimal Props** Focusing on the food itself, rather than cluttering the frame with excessive props, can force attention on light, composition, and the food\'s natural appeal[8].\n\nUnsplash also features general categories for \"Food & Drink\" with thousands of high-resolution photos and \"Food Photography & Styling\" with many free photos that can serve as inspiration[9][10][11][12]. You can find examples of \"brownies in close-up photography\" and \"a close up of a tray of brownies\" directly on Unsplash[13][14].\n\nSources:\n[1] [unsplash.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQERPgc-RhedhAd2cJh6hp_3Fy8yWsb10GCSQTV34dFMlk5-No5-5wg_VvbZR0vTUwVg4yntavHXy2HyxfeaZo8eOvORs_oWzsDsrkzsI0Xm5oAOoqRaCtFuo-ILJZxz3bBrAN-v-PxU4x18Pw==)\n[2] [unsplash.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFirXMYgagX2XdCcvhMqVBCMI_lljOHeW7zoFl_ru3CN53LkC6cEdPAh_ImUm1EfGFiefyCz0UCiu86Dm23v7cuQmx24al_NMLUUq4cHKRcaVOTHMo7OcUlBfcoIPJkooQhqO3TrYhzP10i)\n[3] [unsplash.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGJEc7h4wA1r7XcTUum56x3ybOZqD_RL7MKGInBiA9MPXgmZrp8zTU8mR9_Mq1i0Wf9qX6RAqNN3tDZ0EI-RLAuiB_tG9SE2u30GErUg2ASydTGXOSNgzwVYeOH1npNJbAc)\n[4] [unsplash.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEAYOIEtPeHtUskI6pLeFszMNuMvO0MRAZd4ykUtxi04EKvcl6blaazVTfnJp4Y-iGsoZiSKw3ifETYONXt7eIHdEjyG216cMwYC-6P2Au8QXJM6zmdG5iYYKlT_sI0crk=)\n[5] [unsplash.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHZ31fZjHCGtWxgKwkPA0ba0n2jZqhKG0vfI_8WTUwHHXnm1pICP9IWMys-_NboeGOAqYGmDl9kP2cAYVz7W7wXcMIR7VGF4GH6iDkC9CJXpTJXozvxwQOOrglPstik3nB6qXU=)\n[6] [whiskfullyso.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEO_ZAyTCNinE