    web_documents {
      url: "https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH-4x6ZoQNxCbP11Tb4NpGxFApVEZN5jSrChmfh_FU7uQEG12b7PrBjY1i-zzVjXTx4mBLs7CV2k5OJzM3ZqtT-te2MKaW9XS2jiKPPHuEXzQW7_N2a7i8Zkeea2bA-kqGMEacnXlA5C_pcYCcrE0UViDSsUo2WuFi9xjZI6seGNTtSQZSlE0GiDUoYdXE="
      title: "foodnetwork.com"
    }
    summary: "A search for \"brownie\" or \"brownies\" on Unsplash.com/photos would typically yield a variety of images of this chocolate baked dessert. While direct image results cannot be displayed, information found about brownies indicates they are a chocolate baked dessert bar that can vary in form from fudgy to cakey, often featuring a glossy \"skin\" on the upper crust. Ingredients such as nuts, frosting, or chocolate chips may also be included[1]. A variation using brown sugar and vanilla instead of chocolate is known as a blond brownie or blondie[1].\n\nBrownies were developed in the United States in the late 19th century and gained popularity in the first half of the 20th century[1]. Early recipes for brownies sometimes contained molasses and nuts without chocolate[1]. By 1907, the brownie was well-established in a recognizable form, with adaptations leading to richer, fudgier versions[1].\n\nMany recipes focus on achieving a fudgy, rich, and chewy texture with a crackly top[2][3][4]. Common ingredients include butter, sugar, cocoa powder, eggs, vanilla, salt, and flour, often with the addition of chocolate chips or chopped chocolate[2][5][3][4]. Baking methods often involve melting butter and sugar, then incorporating eggs and dry ingredients, and baking until a toothpick inserted comes out with moist crumbs for a fudgy brownie[2][3].\n\nSources:\n[1] [wikipedia.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFs1TcKfj0Ti4v1_WwxBI-yvX482z6Tjy9Nm_xZ7MeIRI5XaH91yww-LFYUJQnJXXt2TK8ywhPFsEoO03vpK0mu7tFBDtv1yA1owCIqHT2NuiDgLzQClJyYSQoCLpg5dHFtmeSG6nVQ-Ws=)\n[2] [preppykitchen.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEiGNeCqdDOTQ7MUZZkEBceaNLfsvrFopmWWZ_NX0XQhwjZx6Azqb1yCfsQfB1AP1xkrP4bFGVbvy1bL2O9rjHltw1b8mA-zUst-V7IsPd5BrsTs-BRoi34mul88uVj7fmuT_0=)\n[3] [sugarhero.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF0nHUYnzbfXJ9wN37VfMHuEeJOueyIz99ch32N1AtBtKXOvKl_kYWkI2WmTniRy7CSiAb2zS3oCqciakA2QTceRXnuy80SYh88TGcGXfd5R6STamjb_mVFC13F7AiSLDMh82l4HrlVvegaVcNWys4pu34JCsSF)\n[4] [browneyedbaker.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFYkJKaP2P1T4g_3w52TqNmjMhRGE0UZdi_QhMHDfdbZ3ZLzyaWm7D4CcNCjuTrzNEAuT33ZTlbXHBQx9K49aLQg4MrIAFPRpAPK-2TgAYZ_bTpGnXtUPZ2zNtHS8EdvdXlleTvJSzepATi3X-1W3uRFzynBqaTxcddgWTRo2GB)\n[5] [foodnetwork.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH-4x6ZoQNxCbP11Tb4NpGxFApVEZN5jSrChmfh_FU7uQEG12b7PrBjY1i-zzVjXTx4mBLs7CV2k5OJzM3ZqtT-te2MKaW9XS2jiKPPHuEXzQW7_N2a7i8Zkeea2bA-kqGMEacnXlA5C_pcYCcrE0UViDSsUo2WuFi9xjZI6seGNTtSQZSlE0GiDUoYdXE=)"
