    summary: "A search on Unsplash.com for photos related to \"bao\", \"bao buns\", \"steamed buns\", \"korean street food\", \"korean bread\", or \"korean pastry\" would yield numerous images showcasing these food items.\n\n\"Bao buns,\" also known as baozi (\345\214\205\345\255\220), are a staple in Chinese cuisine, characterized by their soft, fluffy, yeast-leavened dough and various sweet or savory fillings like seasoned pork, vegetables, or sweet red bean paste[1]. The term \"bao\" means \"bun\" or \"to wrap\"[1]. There are also \"Lotus Leaf Bao,\" a type of steamed bun often associated with Taiwan, that opens up to hold fillings such as braised pork belly[2].\n\n\"Korean chicken steamed buns\" are also popular, often featuring a sweet, spicy gochujang chicken filling in a soft, fluffy dough[3]. \"Pan-fried steamed buns,\" or Sheng Jian Bao (\347\224\237\347\205\216\345\214\205), are a well-known street food from Shanghai, distinguished by their fluffy texture and a crunchy, pan-fried bottom[4]. These are typically pan-fried until golden on one side and then steamed to cook through[4].\n\nVarious recipes exist for making these buns, often involving flour, yeast, sugar, and a leavening agent[1][2][4]. Some recipes, like those for Korean chicken steamed buns, incorporate both baking powder and yeast to reduce preparation time[3].\n\nSources:\n[1] [whattocooktoday.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHqytHd2CCvzMcKjuOn9u4qnJoEHoAQF7Y1FJcQNl8X_7w6Qdq3Lm7wkdJSgMo51HDq3RpeI5t2AzRiR2tEzsVdoJt-xKauqpWQmWN27_ok0dGoCp_fLcfQ9qZfy7XWNY6nu1i2mCk3K4nAxMuCaZyqadLfIWkV1RY03ydZzQl-HO2QHso=)\n[2] [breadtopia.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFzSLNOXKNYyxQwkUrCyTw18yT3WqZ6Uao5vpT8zUGPsRCzmMJRfbtvJKjHFQUWsCcFXRXBZiWYOhhobmmnt-cQwKJCNDTQ8Tt0HNNnhKYoSayr5T6r55bBq17xVgoqVVq92miwSOFwsQ==)\n[3] [dishedbykate.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGFuhK8EAmEjvAHIDPTcNLaG28GHKm